        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "The integration of artificial intelligence (AI) into education has opened new possibilities for enhancing student learning experiences, particularly in the area of language development. This paper investigates the potential of large language models (LLMs), specifically OpenAI's ChatGPT, to provide meaningful and actionable feedback on student writing in a university setting. Writing proficiency is a cornerstone of academic success, particularly in English as a Second Language (ESL) contexts, where students often face significant challenges in articulating complex ideas in written form. Traditional methods of providing feedback, while effective, are time-consuming and often depend on the availability and expertise of instructors. Automated feedback generated by AI presents an opportunity to alleviate the pressure on educators while offering consistent, timely, and personalized feedback to students. \n\nThis study focuses on first-year university students in Hong Kong, all enrolled in an English-language course aimed at developing foundational writing skills. Through a randomized controlled trial, students were tasked with writing an argumentative essay and revising their work based on the feedback provided. One group received AI-generated feedback, while the control group received conventional feedback from instructors. The goal of the study is to assess the extent to which AI-generated feedback can enhance the quality of student revisions, foster positive learning experiences, and increase student motivation and engagement. In doing so, this research addresses a gap in the literature concerning the effectiveness of generative AI within the specific context of higher education in Hong Kong. \n\nThe rapid advancement of AI technologies has brought about significant improvements in natural language processing (NLP), making tools like ChatGPT capable of generating human-like text and providing detailed feedback on writing tasks. However, while the potential for AI to revolutionize feedback processes in education is promising, empirical evidence on its efficacy within university-level language education remains limited. This study seeks to fill that gap by examining both the quantitative effects of AI-generated feedback on essay scores and the qualitative experiences of students undergoing the revision process. The findings contribute not only to ongoing discussions about the role of AI in education but also to practical considerations for integrating AI tools into ESL writing courses in higher education.",
