      "snippet": {
        "text": "K-12 education emphasizes foundational skills, but higher education demands a more integrated, reflective approach to problem-solving, making this definition particularly relevant for curriculum innovation at this stage. Thus, it is crucial to innovate the curriculum at all levels of the educational system in order to prepare students for the 21st-century socio-economic landscape. Considering this socio-economic landscape, defining critical thinking is a normative enterprise in which appropriate criteria and standards are applied to what students say, do, or write in order to acquire intellectual resources such as foundational knowledge and effective heuristics (Bailin et al., 1999). Examples of such standards that students should demonstrate could be clarity, relevance, accuracy, fairness and logical consistency. \n\nAdditional to teaching critical thinking, there is also a need to be able to assess student success. An issue arises in how to formatively assess critical thinking in an unbiased, consistent manner. There are numerous standardized tools available, such as California Critical Thinking Skills Test (CCTST) and the Health Sciences Reasoning Test (HSRT), but all of them have limitations associated with their use or have limited validation studies performed (Anders et al., 2019). There are also differences in what exact skills these tests assess. For example, the HSRT assesses induction, deduction, analysis, inference, and evaluation skills (Hanlon et al., 2018), compared with the HEIghten Critical Thinking Assessment (HCTA), which assesses analysis and synthesis (Jauregui et al., 2024). \n\nThe literature still debates the wide range of definitions and strategies for incorporating and assessing critical thinking. Nevertheless, it is crucial to continue the discussion and explore its progress. Therefore, this study aimed to gain an understanding of how faculty members perceive critical thinking skills in their classrooms, the methods they use to assess them, how they integrate critical thinking into their respective disciplines, and how important they consider it to be for student success.",
