      "snippet": {
        "text": "Especially considering Black and Wiliam's seminal definition of formative assessment as \"evidence about student achievement [that] is elicited, interpreted, and used by teachers, learners, or their peers, to make decisions about the next steps in instruction\" (1998, p. 9), there is relatively less knowledge on how instructors can use the feedback process to make those decisions about next steps in instruction. This paucity of research is particularly surprising since instructional adjustments based on formative assessments have been found to be associated with learning gains (Black and Wiliam 1998;Burkhardt and Schoenfeld 2019). This is one gap in the larger scholarship on formative assessment and feedback that this article attempts to fill.",
