      "snippet": {
        "text": "Some written comments from students expressed a preference for AIT feedback, highlighting the value of its detailed and structured approach. The expert also noted that HT's handwritten comments were often too brief and unclear, which may have contributed to lower ratings for HT and discrepancies between expert analysis and student perceptions. It is also worth suggesting that the language used for feedback should be more relatable to students, especially for non-native English speakers. \n\nThese findings suggest that AI-generated feedback can be as valuable as human feedback and, in many respects, even superior, aligning with research showing that AI tools offer detailed, timely, and consistent feedback. 10,11 Personalised feedback, a cornerstone of effective teaching, addresses individual learning needs and fosters growth, and AIT has demonstrated the ability to deliver it in multiple formats, tailoring suggestions to enhance clarity, depth, and adherence to marking criteria. AIT's capacity to deliver detailed, personalised feedback far surpasses HT within a short timeframe, given the labour-intensive nature of providing such feedback to multiple students. \n\nThese factors highlight the importance of AI tutors reaching a level of maturity where they can provide feedback as effective as that of expert HT. This capability is particularly valuable for educational institutions facing teacher shortages and in contexts where scalability and consistency are critical, such as large online courses. \n\nWhile this study provides valuable insights into AI-based feedback, several limitations may affect the generalizability of the results. These include the small sample size, the use of only two questions, the focus on a single subject (histology), and the fact that it was conducted at a single site. Additionally, there is potential for student bias, as students may prefer HT, even though the tutor was unknown. Although anonymity was used to mitigate this bias, students' personal preferences for human feedback may have still influenced the objectivity of their responses. \n\nAdditionally, the variability in prompts and potential biases inherited from training data can present some challenges. The accuracy of marks was found to be satisfactory when individual answers were accompanied by the rubric, yet this approach is impractical for large-scale assessments. To optimize AI tools for assessing large volumes of answers, it is recommended to develop the use of standard and fewer prompts.",
