s (RCJs) or judgments of learning (JOLs). RCJs ask students to indicate their confidence in the correctness of their performance on a completed problem-solving task, whereas JOLs ask students to make a predictive judgment of how confident they are regarding their performance on a future problem-solving task. Note that this is a conceptual distinction: in both cases, monitoring accuracy is calculated as the difference between the students' judgment (either RCJ or JOL) and actual performance. \n\nMethodological study features included design and setting.",
