      "snippet": {
        "text": "Art courses in elementary schools or K-6 education1 are popular around the world. In these courses, instructors implement a variety of teaching methods with activities (e.g., group or individual projects, discussions, exhibitions) to foster children's creativity, critical thinking, self-identity, and social competencies [15,16]. Project-based learning 2 Zhiqing et al. \n\n(PBL) is one such method that is extensively applied in art education [2,31,42,62]. This approach engages students in real-world projects and often encourages them to discuss ideas and work together in peer groups. The effectiveness of PBL has been well-documented in fostering students' creativity, autonomy, collaboration, problem-solving skills, and reflective thinking in real-world contexts [10,21,23,45]. \n\nFrom the teachers' perspectives, a typical PBL process in art course can include planning (e.g., topic, question, deadlines), testing (e.g., project progress, facilitation), and reflecting (e.g., presentation, explanation) [21]. This paper focuses on the testing phase of the PLB process, in which students in a small group need to work together to finish a task given by the teachers. During this phase, students gather relevant information, brainstorm ideas, and implement solutions. However, elementary school students may struggle with these tasks due to limited knowledge, teamwork skills, and self-directed learning abilities [8,36]. In offline classroom where there is normally one art teacher in one class, students can query the teacher for support when they face challenges during information seeking and solution ideation. Nevertheless, the teacher's support may not be in time, especially when managing multiple groups with varying needs and abilities [36,56,66]. \n\nRecent advances in Generative AIs (GenAIs) offer a promising alternative to offering in-situ information seeking and ideation support to students in their PBL art courses.",
