      "snippet": {
        "text": "Evaluation and feedback are key links in classroom listening activities to improve teaching quality and students' listening skills. Teachers should design appropriate evaluation criteria for students of different age groups and cognitive levels and provide timely and specific feedback to effectively promote the development of students' listening skills. \n\nFor elementary school students, assessment can focus on their accuracy in listening comprehension and clarity in basic language expression. Teachers should use simple and clear evaluation methods, such as listening to music and selecting pictures, listening to instructions, and doing actions, to accurately understand students' initial performance in listening. When providing feedback, teachers should fully acknowledge students' efforts and progress, use encouraging language to point out their small errors in listening comprehension or language expression and provide specific correction suggestions. \n\nFor middle school students, the evaluation criteria can be more diversified, focusing on the accuracy of listening comprehension, reaction speed, and logical language organization. Teachers can evaluate students by having them listen to short passages, answer questions, listen to dialogues, and complete forms. In the feedback stage, teachers should detail the problems that students have in listening comprehension or language expression and provide specific improvement methods and practice suggestions to help students gradually improve their listening skills. \n\nFor high school students, assessment should focus more on depth and breadth. Teachers can evaluate students' understanding ability, critical thinking ability, and application of academic language in complex listening materials. Students can be evaluated by listening to lectures, news, or academic discussions. When providing feedback, teachers should comprehensively analyze students' listening performance, point out their problems in understanding complex information, grasp the main idea and details, and provide targeted training suggestions to help them improve their listening skills. \n\nThrough such an evaluation and feedback mechanism, teachers can understand students' strengths and weaknesses in listening and provide them with more targeted guidance, effectively promoting the sustained development of students' listening skills. At the same time, this mechanism also helps to stimulate students' interest and enthusiasm for learning, laying a solid foundation for their English learning [11].",
