      "snippet": {
        "text": "Moreover, it points to the need to equip students with AI literacy skills, including an understanding of how GenAI models work and an awareness of their limitations (Walter 2024). developing these skills would enable students to critically assess the quality of AI-generated feedback and interact with these systems more effectively (ding et al. 2023; nikolopoulou 2024). \n\nWhile these findings emphasize the potential of AI-generated feedback to outperform peer assessments in certain contexts, they contrasts with recent work by Banihashem et al. (2024), who found that peer feedback was of higher quality than that generated by AI. One possible explanation for this discrepancy is the difference in participant populations: Banihashem et al. 's study involved graduate students with more experience in academic writing and peer review, whereas the participants in this study were undergraduate students, who may have provided less structured or confident feedback. Additionally, their study focused specifically on essay writing tasks, while the present study examined feedback across multiple assessment components within a broader project. The divergence in findings suggests that student experience levels and task types may influence the relative effectiveness of AI-generated feedback and peer feedback. \n\nTaken together, this study's findings underscore the importance of human judgment in capturing nuanced insights that AI may overlook, and highlight the risks of relying solely on AI for assessment. A balanced approach that combines the strengths of AI-based tools with human evaluation may offer more comprehensive and pedagogically meaningful feedback. By integrating AI with traditional assessment methods, educators can harness the efficiency and structure of automated feedback while preserving the contextual sensitivity, personalization, and instructional alignment that human evaluators provide.",
