        ],
        "openAccessInfo": {
          "license": "CCBY",
          "status": "HYBRID",
          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1007/s43545-024-01003-7?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1007/s43545-024-01003-7, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "This study has found teachers' growing awareness of AI tools and their impacts on education. Findings suggest that the use of advanced AI is changing the context of teaching and learning. For teachers, the emergence of AI in education has both advantages and challenges. One of the benefits is that teachers can leverage AI-based technologies as assistive tools to explore digital materials and incorporate them into their teaching and learning. Moreover, they can create personalised learning, interactive tutoring system, and automatic grading mechanisms. In particular, AI systems provide students with instant feedback on their work (Holmes et al. 2023). Particularly, they can restructure the learning environment so that both teachers and their students can manage to communicate and share ideas without restricting space and time. AI tools can help teachers and students approach problems and ideas in innovative ways, integrate them into their daily activities, and transform the learning experience. Teachers' use of AI in their instructional activities can help students be prepared to use these innovative technologies in their daily lives. But in order to include AI into lessons, teachers must possess the fundamental knowledge of the technology (Ng et al. 2022). Huang and his colleagues (2023) suggest that AI capabilities could be included in teacher development programmes to equip teachers with the essential knowledge to use AI in teaching and learning. In particular, specific guidelines for the ethical use of AI in teaching and learning activities are essential. Additionally, teacher development programmes should incorporate AI literacy to ensure that teachers are aware of the potential and challenges of AI in educational practices. \n\nConcerns regarding AI's impact on education, such as how it might diminish the role of the teacher and put more emphasis on technology, were mentioned by the study's teachers as one of their issues. While AI is capable of understanding human language, it can compose academic essays, solve mathematical puzzles, and develop innovative algorithms-tasks that humans have been performing for generations (Teubner et al. 2023). AI is capable of accomplishing structured learning tasks. It suggests that the standard educational system in use today is ineffective and that teachers must be able to use cutting-edge AI in novel ways. Despite the limited knowledge about AI, teachers in this study emphasised the use of AI tools in conjunction with transformative teaching methods, and that AI tools should be tailored to the specific needs and goals of the classroom.",
        "snippetKind": "body",
        "section": "Conclusion",
