      "snippet": {
        "text": "This design choice may help explain the misalignment between perceived and actual outcomes. Moving forward, future research should develop more robust measures to capture students' gains in conceptual understanding and higher-order thinking, which would provide a more accurate evaluation of the educational impact of AI-assisted learning tools. \n\nThe findings of our work also make a timely contribution to the ongoing debate on whether GenAI tools hinder or enhance students' higher-order thinking skills (e.g., Kosmyna et al., 2025;Stadler et al., 2024). In our study, a substantial proportion of the participants reported gains in problem-solving, critical thinking, and selfreflection as a result of interacting with the tool. Qualitative feedback further suggested that students found the structured, Socratic-style interactions as beneficial for moving beyond surface-level understanding, developing analytical thinking and clarifying logic in programming tasks. Additionally, these benefits were especially salient in responses describing how the tool encouraged students to ask themselves better questions, consider alternatives, and challenge assumptions. These findings align with earlier studies showing that when AI is used as a scaffold, it can enhance critical thinking rather than inhibit it (Fakour & Imani, 2025;Favero et al., 2024). \n\nMoreover, our findings offer a robust proof-of-concept for leveraging GenAI technologies to develop a formative assessment tool that can empower both students and instructors. Participants' reflections on perceived gains in reflective thinking skills suggest that engaging with AI-powered Socratic dialogue created opportunities for students to actively monitor and evaluate their own understanding with personalized feedback and thinking processes based on personalized feedback. By identifying misconceptions and offering step-by-step guidance toward conceptual clarity, the tool functioned as a proactive scaffold for individual learning. Such outcomes align with prior research that has demonstrated the potential of GenAI technologies to foster metacognitive awareness and selfdirected learning (Weng et al. 2024;Yan et al. 2024).",
