      "snippet": {
        "text": "The very first pilot study of CyberScholar, the tool mentioned in this paper, showed that it supported the development of students' writing in several ways. \n\nWhile recent research demonstrates the potential of GenAI tools in writing instruction, other studies also note limitations and ethical concerns, especially in K-12 contexts, which include biases in AI-generated feedback, risks to student privacy, and inequities in access to the technology (Dong, 2023;Echols, 2023;Freiberger & Buchmann, 2024;Li et al., 2023;UNESCO, 2021UNESCO, , 2023)). \n\nThe present paper introduces an additional dimension to the potential of GenAI for writing assistance by aligning AI-generated feedback with teachers' rubrics and classroom learning materials. This research analyzes the proposition of a new --being tested --GenAI tool, CyberScholar, that offers a potentially more consistent and pedagogically grounded method for formative assessment, directly connected to students' writing. The aim is to offer opportunities for collaborative and personalized feedback since the tool can be customized to fit teachers' requests and expectations based on the materials used and the rubric chosen for the evaluation of the specific content area and context. Students are able to present multiple versions of their work and ask the calibrated AI tool to provide feedback. \n\nThe case study approach (Yin, 2014) was particularly suited to this research because it allowed for: an examination of real-world classroom use of GenAI in writing through the assistant tool, an exploration of students' perspectives on writing support and formative feedback, a special attention to the situated nature of writing practices across disciplines. By emphasizing context and meaning-making, the qualitative design supported the study's goal of uncovering how GenAI might influence writing processes, engagement, and outcomes in authentic educational settings. \n\nThe study also followed an agile methodology approach (Martin, 2009), incorporating iterative feedback loops to refine the implementation process, since there is also a component of tool development involved. Each development cycle in each school provided opportunities for participants to suggest adjustments and improvements, ensuring CyberScholar's adaptability to diverse classroom contexts. So this was part of the research pipeline that was a result of the research being conducted.",
