        "text": "Application-level tasks evaluate a model's ability to transfer learned knowledge to practical, contextrich educational settings. These tasks simulate authentic classroom scenarios where students are expected to solve problems, interpret structured information, make pedagogical classifications, or assess open-ended work. The task set spans multimodal dialogue interpretation, quantitative reasoning, and evaluative writing feedback across primary to high school levels. \n\nClassroom classification tasks require models to categorize utterances into instructional roles and are evaluated standard classification accuracy. Essay scoring tasks demand nuanced judgments along multiple dimensions such as coherence, grammar, and content relevance; models are expected to assign scalar scores aligned with expertannotated rubrics. Mathematical problem-solving tasks, ranging from basic arithmetic to multi-step scientific applications, assess models' ability to process numerical and symbolic input in realistic educational formats. Depending on the task structure, responses are evaluated via accuracy for closed-form answers or correlation-based metrics (e.g., QWK, Pearson) for open-ended assessment. \n\nClassroom Dialogue Classification (3-1) This task involves analyzing authentic classroom dialogue transcripts and classifying utterances into pedagogically meaningful categories (e.g., teacher explanation, student elaboration, questioning, or feedback). The dialogues are sourced from real middle school classrooms in subjects such as Information Technology, Mathematics, and Chinese. Original classroom recordings were transcribed to text and annotated by domain experts. The detailed taxonomy used for classification is provided in Appendix E. \n\nPrimary Problem Solving (3-2) The primarylevel application tasks focus on basic quantitative reasoning grounded in everyday life. Problem types include simple arithmetic word problems, timedistance calculations, shopping-related 